+++ b/.standards/STANDARD.md
@@ -0,0 +1,118 @@
+# S2: small Rust modules
+
+**Source:** [`AGENTS.md`](../AGENTS.md), "Avoid large modules": target Rust modules under 500 LoC,
+excluding tests; if a file exceeds roughly 800 LoC, add new functionality in a new module instead of
+extending the existing file.
+
+## The rule as enforced
+
+| Item | Value |
+|---|---|
+| Scope | every `.rs` file under `codex-rs/` in the working tree (tracked, plus untracked files that are not git-ignored) |
+| Measure | every line of the file (newline count, plus an unterminated last line); comments and blanks count |
+| Violation | more than **700** lines (`threshold` in `s2-config.json`; lowered from 800 in the commit after `db82e2f`). 500 is the target, not a violation |
+| Exempt (tests) | any file with a `tests/` directory in its path, files named `tests.rs`, files ending in `_tests.rs` |
+| Exempt (explicit) | paths listed under `exempt` in [`s2-config.json`](./s2-config.json) |
+
+The enforced limit is stricter than the "roughly 800" in AGENTS.md: the team tightened it to 700 so
+files are stopped earlier on the way to the 500 target.
+
+**Ratchet:** a file already over 700 lines may not grow; a file at or under 700 may not cross 700.
+The allowed sizes live in [`s2-baseline.json`](./s2-baseline.json). On pull requests the gate also
+fails if the baseline itself was loosened (a file added, or an allowance raised) relative to the base
+branch, so the baseline can only tighten.
+
+## Commands
+
+Run from the repository root. Only Python 3 and git are needed.
+
+**Report**: every violating file as JSON, largest first:
+
+```sh
+python3 .standards/s2_module_size.py report
+```
+
+Output: `[{"file": "codex-rs/...", "lines": 11183}, ...]`.
+
+**Gate**: exits 1 if a violating file grew or a file newly crossed 700 lines, 0 otherwise. It prints
+`note: SHRANK` / `note: FIXED` when a file shrank or dropped to 700 or below, which means the baseline
+can be tightened:
+
+```sh
+python3 .standards/s2_module_size.py gate
+```
+
+CI ([`.github/workflows/standards-s2.yml`](../.github/workflows/standards-s2.yml)) runs it on every
+pull request with `--base-ref <base sha>`, which adds the baseline-loosening check:
+
+```sh
+python3 .standards/s2_module_size.py gate --base-ref origin/main
+```
+
+**Campaign**: progress toward zero violating files:
+
+```sh
+python3 .standards/s2_module_size.py campaign          # text
+python3 .standards/s2_module_size.py campaign --json   # machine-readable
+```
+
+It prints the campaign's starting number of files (`campaign_start` in the baseline), the current
+number, and the percentage done, `(start - current) / start`. CI writes it to the job summary.
+
+**Current campaign: 229 files over 700 lines, restarted with the threshold change.** The first
+campaign started at 191 files over 800 lines (commit `db82e2f`), and none had been fixed when the
+threshold was lowered (current was still 191). Lowering it to 700 added 38 files (705 to 800 lines)
+to the baseline. Keeping `campaign_start` at 191 would have made the campaign read 0% done with
+229 files to go, and it would only start moving after 38 files were fixed, so `campaign_start` was
+deleted and re-recorded as 229: the campaign measures the standard as it now stands, and no progress
+was lost. If the threshold changes again after files have been fixed, carry the progress over instead:
+set `campaign_start` to the old start plus the number of files the change added, so percent done
+does not jump.
+
+## Updating the baseline
+
+After you shrink or split a file (the gate prints `note:` lines), tighten the baseline in the same
+pull request:
+
+```sh
+python3 .standards/s2_module_size.py update-baseline
+git add .standards/s2-baseline.json
+```
+
+`update-baseline` records every current violation at its current size and keeps `campaign_start`, so
+the campaign percentage is not reset. Never run it to absorb growth: CI rejects a baseline that
+loosened against the base branch.
+
+**Renames:** moving a violating file to a new path shows as `NEW VIOLATION` (and the old path as
+`FIXED`). Split the file while you move it, or add an explicit exemption with a reason (below).
+
+## Changing the standard
+
+Changes to the standard are changes to `.standards/`; review them like code.
+
+- **Change the threshold:** edit `threshold` in `s2-config.json`, then run `update-baseline` and
+  commit both. The gate fails while the config and baseline thresholds differ. Lowering the threshold
+  adds files to the baseline, so CI's loosening check fails on that PR; that is expected and the
+  reviewers should approve it deliberately. If you want the campaign to restart from the new number,
+  delete `campaign_start` from the baseline before running `update-baseline`.
+- **Exempt a file:** add it to `exempt` in `s2-config.json` with the "strong documented reason"
+  AGENTS.md asks for:
+
+  ```json
+  "exempt": {
+    "codex-rs/foo/src/generated.rs": "generated by build tooling; not hand-edited"
+  }
+  ```
+
+  Then run `update-baseline` to drop it from the baseline.
+- **Change the test exemptions or line counting:** edit `is_test_file` or `count_lines` in
+  `s2_module_size.py` and update the table above.
+
+## Files
+
+| File | Purpose |
+|---|---|
+| `.standards/s2_module_size.py` | report, gate, campaign and update-baseline |
+| `.standards/s2-config.json` | threshold, scope root, explicit exemptions |
+| `.standards/s2-baseline.json` | ratchet: allowed size of each existing violation, plus `campaign_start` |
+| `.github/workflows/standards-s2.yml` | runs the gate on pull requests |
